  "contract_plain_english": "For a contaminant carried through a one-dimensional pipe by fluid with constant velocity ū, q(x,t) is its scalar concentration or density [printed page 1, example preceding (1.2)]. When contaminant mass is conserved, its flux is f(q) = ūq and its governing scalar advection equation is q_t + ūq_x = 0 [printed page 4, contaminant example after (1.10), referring to (1.2)]. Conservation balances the rate of change of the quantity inside an interval with flux at its first endpoint minus flux at its second endpoint; it does not require the quantity in that interval to remain constant [printed page 4, (1.10) and its interpretation]. If contaminant mass is not conserved, for example because of chemical reactions, the law must also include source terms, whose form is not specified in this passage [printed page 4, sentence following f(q) = ūq]. The surrounding discussion requires sufficient smoothness to derive the classical differential law from the integral law [printed page 4, Section 1.1.2]."
